#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/id #uuid "418bea54-7fb3-5013-b91c-355a45af08e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11151" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\nx = {y^2}, \\qquad x=\\dfrac{y^2}3, \\qquad y=\\dfrac2x, \\qquad y=\\dfrac4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/d4/d408e7580bef6b1127da124a78df019bca51a55c58968da262540cd45c1658d0.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}, \\qquad v= xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/e6/e61d4153ecfb2fb176c06f67efb13af205b9b43b0ce4791627fcde979d96610e.svg)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u= \\dfrac{y^2}{x}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\quad 2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{x}{3y^2} = -\\dfrac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550327 :question-generator/id #uuid "fa49723a-10c7-5b96-9133-32d3b1b78e99" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186129261.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c2703af-9466-5bfe-b4be-6b83ac22c669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126525} {:answer/id #uuid "cf58a05e-6426-5bb8-8231-daefdfe1b35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126526} {:answer/id #uuid "e030ad9f-a5b5-582f-939f-64d484910356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126527} {:answer/id #uuid "3310ee92-258d-50a0-8117-218100854be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126528} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3f2756-8915-5175-ae43-ff1f5dcb79fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf58a05e-6426-5bb8-8231-daefdfe1b35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126526} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace18637-bd27-5171-b992-40bdb4d2ac91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297a0864-bd1f-5e14-96f0-53d43c9309da" :question/math-academy-id "q-160365" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\,v=y+x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2x-1,\\,y=2x+1,\\,y=1-x,\\,y=2-x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-2x,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-2x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-2x=-1 & ⟹\\, & u=-1 \\\\  & y=2x+1 & ⟹\\, & y-2x=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-x & ⟹\\, & y+x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=2-x & ⟹\\, & y+x=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/21/2116f558e3712a08c13d49238054336dcb60efc7d9602bed220be8115d392be7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2aafe7e-6c91-5d6d-8464-7dba52b30e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126537} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd35985-e84f-5813-8588-98b9055133fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126538} {:answer/id #uuid "d2697d0b-ab20-5ad0-9fbf-426d7b8ac704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126539} {:answer/id #uuid "0d245dc4-6278-59dd-ac0e-565b7ead760b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126540} {:answer/id #uuid "45b03faf-db45-58e5-91f3-c3f8035ce775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126541}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5dc97d-8128-54b5-935f-d17d396d9b2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126536}] :question/id #uuid "af55d298-694f-59ec-ab70-d9760a4b8e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160379" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = \\frac{y}{x^{2}}, v = xy$,\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n$y = x^{2}, y = 2x^{2}, y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{4}{x}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184368e1-d4ec-53a7-b724-26ecbd05d7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371901} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd23822-1245-54b3-b063-1a3e5b75308f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371902} {:answer/id #uuid "04872f3c-0156-57be-a135-c6b36ffb10ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371903} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd73d74-b8b1-5322-8406-42b2b8fdfb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371904} {:answer/id #uuid "8428d403-ae08-501f-9369-f40eb9366d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8428d403-ae08-501f-9369-f40eb9366d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371905} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba19557e-03f5-528c-ad20-5f4ba059cdd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5d19e11-2889-5a4f-a620-f1b6f728a9db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160870" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=3x-2,\\,y=3x+2,\\,y=1-2x,\\,y=3-2x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-3x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=3x+2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+2x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=3-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9c17c81b4abc0d4ebd1eadfe951b0eb0c2674d9eb07b785e7fd972fd00e513ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d59625-eac9-589e-ab14-341551eea08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371915} {:answer/id #uuid "7b889d37-5d45-5818-9d27-40379a4533ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371916} {:answer/id #uuid "b06248ff-2151-5554-b5af-ab842ba5705a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371917} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e1a6f5-fc15-5138-88ce-fdee68bb88d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371918} {:answer/id #uuid "5b52d6ac-ffd9-5f34-ba9e-30bc4a86d030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b889d37-5d45-5818-9d27-40379a4533ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371916} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ac9b17-c604-511f-9b48-3b87989affec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75940fb8-f294-5aab-8f7b-cfe4e75211a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160891" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b2bbcb8-c26d-5006-a73c-bbb060d2cd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491568} {:answer/id #uuid "aba8c641-4582-5c5c-96f9-0d22c8330b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491753} {:answer/id #uuid "d6091ef9-6288-59c1-9465-7c82caaeb2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491819} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb91197-701b-57ed-96ae-159bf0a24d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491858} {:answer/id #uuid "edc9ced6-e6c7-5f1d-be8e-a811c81b1ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b2bbcb8-c26d-5006-a73c-bbb060d2cd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491568} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c5a083c-f900-50bb-ab0f-b4084bf566f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "018796a4-bd8d-5016-90ff-54138b129ff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-160875" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=1+\\frac{x}{2},\\,y=-1+\\frac{x}{2},\\,y=x-1,\\,y=x-2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=2y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=-1+\\frac{x}{2} & ⟹\\, & 2y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y-x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=x-1 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-1 & ⟹\\, & v=-1 \\\\  & y=x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-x=-2 & ⟹\\, & v=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e9/e9ad855bcb5133e9a51d990ea25d0e23c8da88c59e15825f3ea1bfc47335dc33.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f427ed-2d2a-5dfa-8945-d1b3f1c00922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491456} {:answer/id #uuid "3d405b8d-b749-5fcf-9281-fe13658b158d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491572} {:answer/id #uuid "947befcc-fa3a-58ec-8c95-d53e4ad8cc01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491708} {:answer/id #uuid "c853fb0b-11f2-5a9b-a138-764447d95351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491794} {:answer/id #uuid "c9995f65-1329-5eb2-aafe-12e03a218173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9995f65-1329-5eb2-aafe-12e03a218173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491797} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3443c26-bcc5-576e-a59c-ebc351a5027e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d01b49-3d16-5ae7-a700-51548665fd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160893" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2},\\,y=3{x}^{2},\\,x={y}^{2},\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=3{x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x={y}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/26/26eaba24af54a40f59f92148c145285ba4097ce765aee8cfc9a6241ff88b9498.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{3{y}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3{u}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}+1)\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab4b08a-d977-567f-a04e-d43ed9d2d0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491542} {:answer/id #uuid "94243889-413b-535c-af6b-ce9f8dfb1709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491706} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ac3fa8-e528-579d-ad93-0c93644320df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491767} {:answer/id #uuid "e10f8c74-4f85-5c66-896d-c5e8857bd026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491836} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ff714-7cca-5ae3-9331-6463299a095b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab4b08a-d977-567f-a04e-d43ed9d2d0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491542} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fcb4a03-8da4-5422-b391-06e26dc1be4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcce5a2c-ee13-5e17-858d-468f144adb26" :question/math-academy-id "q-160892" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3},\\,y=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b6a044-942f-5f80-b14d-73ed378bca93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550262} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a2da4d-6210-5be9-81a0-ecf8b36f9fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550304} {:answer/id #uuid "d35e7323-a3a8-5279-b564-2450b3823623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550312} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ed603a-a6d1-59ec-a92d-3cb73aaa6f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550319} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0d6cf6-2603-558d-a00a-f5ea02b21f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1a2da4d-6210-5be9-81a0-ecf8b36f9fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550304} :answer-field/id #uuid "adeaaac1-383a-5cad-9711-2562527f01ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "004c532d-124d-5053-9b4b-e5607aeffc93" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=4 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{2}{5 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{7}{4 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{2}{5 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=5 & ⟹\\, & u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d3dd7067bbcb0a6e3c901d12a2795500e192594a14e7f1373dd325e4fd270363.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091b43cb-3824-5278-b02a-80954580abf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550253} {:answer/id #uuid "0a734b23-70c8-5a87-b181-df0896bb13b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550254} {:answer/id #uuid "178d842d-884a-503a-aed8-8f5a489cdee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550257} {:answer/id #uuid "4e095de2-8e72-5c9f-a025-ba50adefeaf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550273} {:answer/id #uuid "788699c0-c7c8-57d6-9abc-ef6f6c7c2cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "178d842d-884a-503a-aed8-8f5a489cdee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550257} :answer-field/id #uuid "783e65fb-53de-5f75-a984-ad63c97517df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f546348-0394-5bd9-acc6-4dd4084bdee8" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=2 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{3}{x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 6,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/15/15f5caab85f05d666bb383692bad6b67d04da853f1412505a62ba6aba7ce36d6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{8 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{8 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{8 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{6}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 6-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0904b7b1-cfa8-5ae6-aa60-5aca7ae33f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550252} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9f8a2d-b59e-5889-a32b-9f1d2fac5c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + 7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550271} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6f989e-1b5b-5f7d-97a4-2ef1d6359863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(21 \\right)}}{2} + 3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550283} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc730c1-3ddc-5798-aa10-5faa26d2866e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550294} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bf27c2-7f80-5b22-9f0e-84d04ea44933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fc730c1-3ddc-5798-aa10-5faa26d2866e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550294} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bcfa681-838b-590c-8d5c-00b8d4d36aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fce4d70-31bd-5ea0-bd52-007df134c134" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\qquad v=x y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=3 x^{2},\\qquad y=7 x^{2},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{8}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\,v=x y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{x^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=7 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=7 & ⟹\\, & u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{8}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=8 & ⟹\\, & v=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,1\\le v\\le 8\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/6c/6cd1c5b38643b6c5e0ceded7b3cae3d06e51135f84fb9598a129e8627b55c14a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 y}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3 y}=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3 u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{7}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{3}^{7}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{8}1\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 3)\\cdot (8-1) \\\\  & =- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34eda6cd-67a8-5208-9e75-fe5046749335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550266} {:answer/id #uuid "51907818-a2e8-522c-aa69-39eb4bf60dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550276} {:answer/id #uuid "58e3803f-728c-5865-b3dc-3ad15dd8df31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550280} {:answer/id #uuid "a0633187-d739-5c3d-8142-90bf6a8cd65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(15 \\right)}}{2} + \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550301} {:answer/id #uuid "dea6270f-e136-5864-8dee-bf3860074050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e3803f-728c-5865-b3dc-3ad15dd8df31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27887e3-37e9-5379-9335-951ca05cfc34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6524cd37-7830-594b-a844-cca2bae3152d" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\qquad v=3 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{3}{8 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{5}{3 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=3 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{5}{3 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 8,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/80/80a923224b455c6f8cfa17a7099c1bb86206a115845954af6123bfb6b3ef5138.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 9 x^{2} y & 3 x^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{18 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{18 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{18 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{8}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{8}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 5-\\ln 3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e7b4f0-779a-5592-82fe-d0c0df5c9448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550256} {:answer/id #uuid "29655adf-66c1-523f-b8f7-b67feb1b58bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550264} {:answer/id #uuid "57adb191-6307-50ae-87c3-b23763d25030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550278} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4e10bf-6c08-5075-aca9-e62df0216285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550293} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e41a24-8fa6-5600-b8a5-7d2b62d4d248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4e41a24-8fa6-5600-b8a5-7d2b62d4d248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550314} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f147d4-75e8-5313-8ada-be150ea18f49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86313c0f-17d0-58ab-b4b7-aaf7ffda4b59" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=4 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x - 2,\\qquad y=2 x + 2,\\qquad y=2 - 4 x,\\qquad y=4 - 4 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=4 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/434447c0d9ece418e17a0ac3f0cc044faf76ba5689ed692fc3408cb78501326c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3838494d-4bc0-57ba-8752-ecc004226996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550268} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef72dfb-cff7-5042-8cb9-d26a99b2f68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550269} {:answer/id #uuid "5140b796-9850-5bf3-9ac2-45cc99ff75ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550275} {:answer/id #uuid "c8aafbcd-c95b-5422-8726-47cc5dbfbe4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550307} {:answer/id #uuid "f096027c-d8bd-5a97-9ddf-45dab5ab5bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3838494d-4bc0-57ba-8752-ecc004226996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550268} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcf5418-3547-52bd-81b4-32f2ae8aea43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e3bead7-3041-5d6a-a3c9-e99ea7492182" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{x y},\\qquad v=x^{2} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{8 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{4}{x^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{x y},\\,v=x^{2} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x^{2} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 2 x y & x^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=y=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{x^{2} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2386a1bb-f698-5731-ac56-eae73d9c2526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550261} {:answer/id #uuid "364f4a6e-3884-5e11-898e-195e0f488998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550267} {:answer/id #uuid "42dd8959-7c57-53cb-96d3-5073891634e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550270} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d24d64-b125-59c2-9c5e-b2e0878caeb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550303} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e81a80-bfe3-51d9-993d-f8507bc8d457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e81a80-bfe3-51d9-993d-f8507bc8d457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550317} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4d613b-bc83-54c0-a0e4-d701b6438ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9eb1a8f5-9688-520c-9d88-de87948baa7e" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x,\\qquad y=2 x + 2,\\qquad y=3 - x,\\qquad y=4 - x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=4 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,3\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2c3cb5e8b7c7448b5f90e372e20e6ff2bd0e547dd085d34ac3103178083ce8c2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{3}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012e1875-ab96-5a40-b850-8d7642320250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550251} {:answer/id #uuid "1f603556-ac3a-53ce-886e-ebae835e98e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550259} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbb1a99-4a86-56b6-a41b-dd3b2bed1c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550260} {:answer/id #uuid "d75c08a4-2365-566a-a3f8-bdf61e29faf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550316} {:answer/id #uuid "fef4672b-8b6b-5412-ad9f-f6bbf1aa9954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "012e1875-ab96-5a40-b850-8d7642320250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550251} :answer-field/id #uuid "58740935-52c9-58bc-be07-3f8d77109e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ccb83066-0219-5269-b997-9cdb213c5816" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=4 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c9946da-c8fb-5598-9310-0635636e2435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550291} {:answer/id #uuid "9346b182-534f-5768-a92d-14cb708091d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550297} {:answer/id #uuid "c43cb67e-0e07-53ac-bd2f-e4b46856febf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{6} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550305} {:answer/id #uuid "f41b3a53-1513-5d90-a29e-c0d94361791b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{8} + \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550322} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5eb5b1-cb2e-5bed-a14f-bed3327759bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5eb5b1-cb2e-5bed-a14f-bed3327759bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550326} :answer-field/id #uuid "92f58760-2c63-5481-aed7-155b18d6271e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cece6418-e25b-52c0-84e8-9f3f46d2fb2f" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\qquad v=2 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{7}{2 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=9 & ⟹\\, & u=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{2 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 9,\\,2\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/07/075e4196e1ad4b6dafc70878555598bac64e8880b2c152d61c2a6437acbaaceb.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{12 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{12 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{12 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{4}^{9}\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e2bf6e-19ed-5858-98ed-6d2551df3a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550255} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7f9d50-f67c-5a4a-9290-79eae5d25588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550287} {:answer/id #uuid "92b6032b-6b22-566f-b2c5-42119f8b2493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550295} {:answer/id #uuid "95eca1f7-296e-5899-ba07-f57fd8732853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550298} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a9b54e-4e72-5767-8a68-557209fb6526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e2bf6e-19ed-5858-98ed-6d2551df3a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550255} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f9d0170-b331-569d-b9e7-07729838c80b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1dac97a-39b4-5865-ae85-b58196c1a98b" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=3 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x - 4,\\qquad y=2 x + 4,\\qquad y=3 - 3 x,\\qquad y=5 - 3 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=3 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-4 & ⟹\\, & u=-4 \\\\  & y=2 x + 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=5 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/438f7c0ee42f63a596ff65ae9c2b354c93bb2f97ad4d7b5b0a1a161a3c5d173b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{5}=- \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87fa627d-b1ee-5f28-a910-da260acc566c" :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241c0011-aeb6-5715-867f-08dbc7b8365c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550263} {:answer/id #uuid "4d801f08-706a-54dd-8726-8efe2ad05c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550272} {:answer/id #uuid "6d454b96-ad9a-520e-8e70-88ce8979f324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550284} {:answer/id #uuid "8027fd76-5626-52e3-8da9-e75dde48cfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550288} {:answer/id #uuid "8b509bef-5cb6-5a5d-9aef-179ae2302238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8027fd76-5626-52e3-8da9-e75dde48cfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550288} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13d0a1a-602a-5493-aa9b-b98af622ae11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d39c0daa-7f15-5653-beed-dc484aaa4d7c" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=4 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x,\\qquad y=2 x + 3,\\qquad y=1 - 4 x,\\qquad y=5 - 4 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 3 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=5 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d6/d605500af69b1a828496810a93a084b065ec92ebd99b03cbf14a37b03840dd31.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Given"}]}
